.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Search through quadrants, the local part of a forest, or the partition\&. 

This file provides several helper functions and a couple highlevel recursive search algorithms\&. These can be used to search for a collection of user-defined 'points' through the forest\&. There are three flavors of the main search algorithm:
.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fBp8est_search_local\fP
.PP
This function examines the processor-local part of the refinement tree\&. It proceeds top-down along all subtrees that have at least one local leaf\&. Non-local subtrees are ignored in an optimized way\&. Use this function to compare points against the local branches and leaves\&.
.IP "2." 4
\fBp8est_search_partition\fP
.PP
This function examines the parallel partition that is known to all processors without knowing about actual leaves on remote processors\&. Use this to find the processors relevant for a collection of points, which can then be used to send each point to its assigned processor\&.
.IP "3." 4
\fBp8est_search_all\fP
.PP
This function combines the first two into one algorithm\&. Note that when the parallel partition is not of interest, \fBp8est_search_local\fP is recommended instead since it employs optimizations that are only possible on the local processor\&. 
.PP

.SH "Typedef Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "typedef int(* p8est_search_all_t) (\fBp8est_t\fP *\fBp4est\fP, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP which_tree, \fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP *quadrant, int pfirst, int plast, \fBp4est_locidx_t\fP local_num, void *point)"

.PP
Callback function for the top-down search through the whole forest\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP The forest to search\&. We recurse through the trees one after another\&. 
.br
\fIwhich_tree\fP The current tree number\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant\fP The current quadrant in the recursion\&. This quadrant is either a non-leaf tree branch or a leaf\&. If the quadrant is contained in the local partition, we know which, otherwise we don't\&. Let us first consider the situation when \fBquadrant\fP is local, which is indicated by both \fBpfirst\fP and \fBplast\fP being equal to \fBp4est->mpirank\fP\&. Then the parameter \fBlocal_num\fP is negative for non-leaves and the number of the quadrant as a leaf in local storage otherwise\&. Only if the quadrant is a local leaf, it points to the actual local storage and can be used to access user data etc\&., and the recursion terminates\&. The other possibility is that \fBpfirst\fP < \fBplast\fP, in which case we proceed with the recursion, or both are equal to the same remote rank, in which case the recursion terminates\&. Either way, the quadrant is not from local forest storage\&.
.br
\fIpfirst\fP The lowest processor that owns part of \fBquadrant\fP\&. Guaranteed to be non-empty\&. 
.br
\fIplast\fP The highest processor that owns part of \fBquadrant\fP\&. Guaranteed to be non-empty\&. 
.br
\fIlocal_num\fP If \fBquadrant\fP is a local leaf, this number is the index of the leaf in local quadrant storage\&. Else, this is a negative value\&.
.br
\fIpoint\fP User-defined representation of a point\&. This parameter distinguishes two uses of the callback\&. For each quadrant, the callback is first called with a NULL point, and if this callback returns true, once for each point tracked in this branch\&. The return value for a point determines whether it shall be tracked further down the branch or not, and has no effect on a local leaf\&. The call with a NULL point is intended to prepare quadrant-related search meta data that is common to all points, and/or to efficiently terminate the recursion for all points in the branch in one call\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
If false, the recursion at \fBquadrant\fP terminates\&. If true, it continues if \fBpfirst\fP < \fBplast\fP or if they are both equal to \fBp4est->mpirank\fP and the recursion has not reached a leaf yet\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "typedef int(* p8est_search_local_t) (\fBp8est_t\fP *\fBp4est\fP, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP which_tree, \fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP *quadrant, \fBp4est_locidx_t\fP local_num, void *point)"

.PP
Callback function to query the match of a 'point' with a quadrant\&. This function can be called in two roles: Per-quadrant, in which case the parameter \fBpoint\fP is NULL, or per-point, possibly many times per quadrant\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP The forest to be queried\&. 
.br
\fIwhich_tree\fP The tree id under consideration\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant\fP The quadrant under consideration\&. This quadrant may be coarser than the quadrants that are contained in the forest (an ancestor), in which case it is a temporary variable and not part of the forest storage\&. Otherwise, it is a leaf and points directly into the forest storage\&. 
.br
\fIlocal_num\fP If the quadrant is not a leaf, this is < 0\&. Otherwise it is the (non-negative) index of the quadrant relative to the processor-local storage\&. 
.br
\fIpoint\fP Representation of a 'point'; user-defined\&. If \fBpoint\fP is NULL, the callback may be used to prepare quadrant-related search meta data\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
If \fBpoint\fP is NULL, true if the search confined to \fBquadrant\fP should be executed, false to skip it\&. Else, true if point may be contained in the quadrant and false otherwise; the return value has no effect on a leaf\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "typedef int(* p8est_search_partition_t) (\fBp8est_t\fP *\fBp4est\fP, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP which_tree, \fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP *quadrant, int pfirst, int plast, void *point)"

.PP
Callback function for the partition recursion\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP The forest to traverse\&. Its local quadrants are never accessed\&. 
.br
\fIwhich_tree\fP The tree number under consideration\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant\fP This quadrant is not from local forest storage, and its user data is undefined\&. It represents the branch of the forest in the top-down recursion\&. 
.br
\fIpfirst\fP The lowest processor that owns part of \fBquadrant\fP\&. Guaranteed to be non-empty\&. 
.br
\fIplast\fP The highest processor that owns part of \fBquadrant\fP\&. Guaranteed to be non-empty\&. If this is equal to \fBpfirst\fP, then the recursion will stop for \fBquadrant's\fP branch after this function returns\&. 
.br
\fIpoint\fP Pointer to a user-defined point object\&. If called per-quadrant, this is NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
If false, the recursion at quadrant is terminated\&. If true, it continues if \fBpfirst\fP < \fBplast\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "typedef int(* p8est_search_reorder_t) (\fBp8est_t\fP *\fBp4est\fP, sc_array_t *quadrants, sc_array_t *indices)"

.PP
Callback function to query, reorder, and reduce a set of quadrants\&. It receives an array of quadrants and an array of array indices on input\&. On output, the array of quadrants is unmodified but the indices may be\&. This function may permute the indices and/or choose a subset\&. Subsetting is effected by resizing the index array\&. Note to resize only before or after, but not during eventual sorting, since resizing may reallocate and thus move the array memory\&. Indices must remain a permutation\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP The forest to be queried\&. 
.br
\fIquadrants\fP The quadrant array under consideration, each with valid coordinates and level\&. The user data piggy1 field of each quadrant contains its tree number\&. Sorted ascending\&. 
.br
\fIindices\fP This array holds \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP types\&. Sorted ascending on input\&. May be permuted and subset by this function\&. It is explicitly allowed to resize it to a smaller length\&. An output length of zero stops recursion\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Return false to break the search recursion\&. 
.RE
.PP

