.SS "void p8est_find_partition (const int num_procs, \fBp4est_gloidx_t\fP * search_in, \fBp4est_gloidx_t\fP my_begin, \fBp4est_gloidx_t\fP my_end, \fBp4est_gloidx_t\fP * begin, \fBp4est_gloidx_t\fP * end)"

.PP
Binary search in partition array\&. Given two targets \fImy_begin\fP and \fImy_end\fP, find offsets such that \fCsearch_in[begin] >= my_begin\fP, \fCmy_end <= search_in[end]\fP\&. If more than one index satisfies the conditions, then the minimal index is the result\&. If there is no index that satisfies the conditions, then \fIbegin\fP and \fIend\fP are tried to set equal such that \fCsearch_in[begin] >= my_end\fP\&. If \fImy_begin\fP is less or equal than the smallest value of \fIsearch_in\fP \fIbegin\fP is set to 0 and if \fImy_end\fP is bigger or equal than the largest value of \fIsearch_in\fP \fIend\fP is set to \fInum_procs\fP - 1\&. If none of the above conditions is satisfied, the output is not well defined\&. We require `my_begin <= my_begin'\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fInum_procs\fP Number of processes to get the length of \fIsearch_in\fP\&. 
.br
\fIsearch_in\fP The sorted array (ascending) in that the function will search\&. If \fCk\fP indexes search_in, then \fC0 <= k < num_procs\fP\&. 
.br
\fImy_begin\fP The first target that defines the start of the search window\&. 
.br
\fImy_end\fP The second target that defines the end of the search window\&. 
.br
\fIbegin\fP The first offset such that \fCsearch_in[begin] >= my_begin\fP\&. 
.br
\fIend\fP The second offset such that \fCmy_end <= search_in[end]\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP* p8est_find_quadrant_cumulative (\fBp8est_t\fP * p8est, \fBp4est_locidx_t\fP cumulative_id, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP * which_tree, \fBp4est_locidx_t\fP * quadrant_id)"

.PP
Search a local quadrant by its cumulative number in the forest\&. We perform a binary search over the processor-local trees, which means that it is advisable NOT to use this function if possible, and to try to maintain O(1) tree context information in the calling code\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp8est\fP\fP Forest to work with\&. 
.br
\fIcumulative_id\fP Cumulative index over all trees of quadrant\&. 
.br
\fIwhich_tree\fP If not NULL, the input value can be -1 or an initial guess for the quadrant's tree\&. An initial guess must be the index of a nonempty local tree\&. Output is the tree of returned quadrant\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant_id\fP If not NULL, the number of quadrant in tree\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The identified quadrant\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t p8est_find_range_boundaries (\fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP * lq, \fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP * uq, int level, int faces[], int edges[], int corners[])"

.PP
Find the boundary points touched by a range of quadrants\&. Given two smallest quadrants, \fBlq\fP and \fBuq\fP, that mark the first and the last quadrant in a range of quadrants, determine which portions of the tree boundary the range touches\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIlq\fP The smallest quadrant at the start of the range: if NULL, the tree's first quadrant is taken to be the start of the range\&. 
.br
\fIuq\fP The smallest quadrant at the end of the range: if NULL, the tree's last quadrant is taken to be the end of the range\&. 
.br
\fIlevel\fP The level of the containing quadrant whose boundaries are tested: 0 if we want to test the boundaries of the whole tree\&. 
.br
\fIfaces\fP An array of size 6 that is filled: faces[i] is true if the range touches that face\&. 
.br
\fIedges\fP An array of size 12 that is filled: edges[i] is true if the range touches that edge\&. 
.br
\fIcorners\fP An array of size 8 that is filled: corners[i] is true if the range touches that corner\&. \fBfaces\fP, \fBedges\fP or \fBcorners\fP may be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Returns an int32_t encoded with the same information in \fBfaces\fP, \fBedges\fP and \fBcorners:\fP the first (least) six bits represent the six faces, the next twelve bits represent the twelve edges, the next eight bits represent the eight corners\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void p8est_search (\fBp8est_t\fP * p4est, \fBp8est_search_query_t\fP quadrant_fn, \fBp8est_search_query_t\fP point_fn, sc_array_t * points)"

.PP
This function is provided for backwards compatibility\&. We call \fBp8est_search_local\fP with call_post = 0\&. 
.SS "void p8est_search_all (\fBp8est_t\fP * p4est, int call_post, \fBp8est_search_all_t\fP quadrant_fn, \fBp8est_search_all_t\fP point_fn, sc_array_t * points)"

.PP
Perform a top-down search on the whole forest\&. This function combines the functionality of \fBp8est_search_local\fP and \fBp8est_search_partition\fP; their documentation applies for the most part\&.
.PP
The recursion proceeds from the root quadrant of each tree until (a) we encounter a remote quadrant that covers only one processor, or (b) we encounter a local leaf quadrant\&. In other words, we proceed with the recursion into a quadrant's children if (a) the quadrant is split between two or more processors, no matter whether one of them is the calling processor or not, or (b) if the quadrant is on the local processor but we have not reached a leaf yet\&.
.PP
The search can track one or more points, which are abstract placeholders\&. They are matched against the quadrants traversed using a callback function\&. The result of the callback function can be used to stop a recursion early\&. The user determines how a point is interpreted, we only pass it around\&.
.PP
Note that in the remote case (a), we may terminate the recursion even if the quadrant is not a leaf, which we have no means of knowing\&. Still, this case is sufficient to determine the processor ownership of a point\&.
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
This is a very powerful function that can become slow if not used carefully\&.
.PP
As with the two other search functions in this file, calling it once with many points is generally much faster than calling it once for each point\&. Using multiple points also allows for a per-quadrant termination of the recursion in addition to a more costly per-point termination\&.
.PP
This function works fine when used for the special cases that either the partition or the local quadrants are not of interest\&. However, in the case of querying only local information we expect that \fBp8est_search_local\fP will be faster since it employs specific local optimizations\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP The forest to be searched\&. 
.br
\fIcall_post\fP If true, call quadrant callback both pre and post point callback, in both cases before recursion (!)\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant_fn\fP Executed once for each quadrant that is entered\&. If the callback returns false, this quadrant and its descendants are excluded from the search, and the points in this branch are not queried further\&. Its \fBpoint\fP argument is always NULL\&. Callback may be NULL in which case it is ignored\&. 
.br
\fIpoint_fn\fP Executed once for each point that is relevant for a quadrant of the search\&. If it returns true, the point is tracked further down that branch, else it is discarded from the queries for the children\&. If \fBpoints\fP is not NULL, this callback must be not NULL\&. If \fBpoints\fP is NULL, it is not called\&. 
.br
\fIpoints\fP User-defined array of points\&. We do not interpret a point, just pass it into the callbacks\&. If NULL, only the \fBquadrant_fn\fP callback is executed\&. If that is NULL, the whole function noops\&. If not NULL, the \fBpoint_fn\fP is called on its members during the search\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void p8est_search_local (\fBp8est_t\fP * p4est, int call_post, \fBp8est_search_local_t\fP quadrant_fn, \fBp8est_search_local_t\fP point_fn, sc_array_t * points)"

.PP
Search through the local part of a forest\&. The search is especially efficient if multiple targets, called 'points' below, are searched for simultaneously\&.
.PP
The search runs over all local quadrants and proceeds recursively top-down\&. For each tree, it may start at the root of that tree, or further down at the root of the subtree that contains all of the tree's local quadrants\&. Likewise, some intermediate levels in the recursion may be skipped if the processor-local part is contained in a single deeper subtree\&. The outer loop is thus a depth-first, processor-local forest traversal\&. Each quadrant in that loop either is a leaf, or a (direct or indirect) strict ancestor of a leaf\&. On entering a new quadrant, a user-provided quadrant-callback is executed\&.
.PP
As a convenience, the user may provide anonymous 'points' that are tracked down the forest\&. This way one search call may be used for multiple targets\&. The set of points that potentially matches a given quadrant diminishes from the root down to the leaves: For each quadrant, an inner loop over the potentially matching points executes a point-callback for each candidate that determines whether the point may be a match\&. If not, it is discarded in the current branch, otherwise it is passed to the next deeper level\&. The callback is allowed to return true for the same point and more than one quadrant; in this case more than one matching quadrant may be identified\&. The callback is also allowed to return false for all children of a quadrant that it returned true for earlier\&. If the point callback returns false for all points relevant to a quadrant, the recursion stops\&. The points can really be anything, \fBp4est\fP does not perform any interpretation, just passes the pointer along to the callback function\&.
.PP
If points are present and the first quadrant callback returned true, we execute it a second time after calling the point callback for all current points\&. This can be used to gather and postprocess information about the points more easily\&. If it returns false, the recursion stops\&.
.PP
If the points are a NULL array, they are ignored and the recursion proceeds by querying the per-quadrant callback\&. If the points are not NULL but an empty array, the recursion will stop immediately!
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP The forest to be searched\&. 
.br
\fIcall_post\fP If true, call quadrant callback both pre and post point callback, in both cases before recursion (!)\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant_fn\fP Executed once when a quadrant is entered, and once when it is left (the second time only if points are present and the first call returned true)\&. This quadrant is always local, if not completely than at least one descendant of it\&. If the callback returns false, this quadrant and its descendants are excluded from the search recursion\&. Its \fBpoint\fP argument is always NULL\&. Callback may be NULL in which case it is ignored\&. 
.br
\fIpoint_fn\fP If \fBpoints\fP is not NULL, must be not NULL\&. Shall return true for any possible matching point\&. If \fBpoints\fP is NULL, this callback is ignored\&. 
.br
\fIpoints\fP User-defined array of 'points'\&. If NULL, only the \fBquadrant_fn\fP callback is executed\&. If that is NULL, this function noops\&. If not NULL, the \fBpoint_fn\fP is called on its members during the search\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void p8est_search_partition (\fBp8est_t\fP * p4est, int call_post, \fBp8est_search_partition_t\fP quadrant_fn, \fBp8est_search_partition_t\fP point_fn, sc_array_t * points)"

.PP
Traverse the global partition top-down\&. We proceed top-down through the partition, identically on all processors except for the results of two user-provided callbacks\&. The recursion will only go down branches that are split between multiple processors\&. The callback functions can be used to stop a branch recursion even for split branches\&. This function offers the option to search for arbitrary user-defined points analogously to \fBp8est_search_local\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP The forest to traverse\&. Its local quadrants are never accessed\&. 
.br
\fIcall_post\fP If true, call quadrant callback both pre and post point callback, in both cases before recursion (!)\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant_fn\fP This function controls the recursion, which only continues deeper if this callback returns true for a branch quadrant\&. It is allowed to set this to NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpoint_fn\fP This function decides per-point whether it is followed down the recursion\&. Must be non-NULL if \fBpoints\fP are not NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpoints\fP User-provided array of \fBpoints\fP that are passed to the callback \fBpoint_fn\fP\&. See \fBp8est_search_local\fP for details\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void p8est_search_partition_gfp (const \fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP * gfp, int nmemb, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP num_trees, int call_post, void * user, \fBp8est_search_partition_t\fP quadrant_fn, \fBp8est_search_partition_t\fP point_fn, sc_array_t * points)"

.PP
Traverse some given global partition top-down\&. The partition can be that of any \fBp4est\fP, not necessarily known to the caller\&. This is not a collective function\&. It does not communicate\&. We proceed top-down through the partition, identically on all processors except for the results of two user-provided callbacks\&. The recursion will only go down branches that are split between multiple processors\&. The callback functions can be used to stop a branch recursion even for split branches\&. This function offers the option to search for arbitrary user-defined points analogously to \fBp8est_search_local\fP\&. This function is similar to \fBp8est_search_partition_gfx\fP, but does not require the \fBp4est_gloidx_t\fP array gfq\&. If gfq is available, using \fBp8est_search_partition_gfx\fP is recommended, because it is slightly faster\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIgfp\fP Partition position to traverse\&. Length \fInmemb\fP + 1\&. 
.br
\fInmemb\fP Number of processors encoded in \fIgfp\fP (plus one)\&. 
.br
\fInum_trees\fP Tree number must match the contents of \fIgfp\fP\&. 
.br
\fIcall_post\fP If true, call quadrant callback both pre and post point callback, in both cases before recursion (!)\&. 
.br
\fIuser\fP We pass a dummy \fBp4est\fP to the callbacks whose only valid element is its user_pointer set to \fIuser\fP\&. 
.br
\fIquadrant_fn\fP This function controls the recursion, which only continues deeper if this callback returns true for a branch quadrant\&. It is allowed to set this to NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpoint_fn\fP This function decides per-point whether it is followed down the recursion\&. Must be non-NULL if \fBpoints\fP are not NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpoints\fP User-provided array of \fBpoints\fP that are passed to the callback \fBpoint_fn\fP\&. See \fBp8est_search_local\fP for details\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void p8est_search_partition_gfx (const \fBp4est_gloidx_t\fP * gfq, const \fBp8est_quadrant_t\fP * gfp, int nmemb, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP num_trees, int call_post, void * user, \fBp8est_search_partition_t\fP quadrant_fn, \fBp8est_search_partition_t\fP point_fn, sc_array_t * points)"

